<h1>CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION</h1>

<h2>1.1 Background and Motivation</h2>
<p>Public bus transportation systems constitute the backbone of urban and regional mobility. Bus depots operate as the operational nerve centers responsible for maintaining fleet assets, dispatching daily schedules, assigning operating crews (drivers and conductors), and ensuring en-route safety. Historically, depot administration has relied on manual paper registers, physical logbooks, or disjointed spreadsheets. Under such manual systems, route assignments are prone to scheduling errors, driver misallocations, and complete lack of real-time visibility once buses leave the depot gate.</p>
<p>A critical operational hazard in transit operations is <strong>bus bunching</strong> (also known as bus clumping or platooning). Bus bunching occurs when two or more transit vehicles running on the same route lose their planned headway interval. If a leading bus experiences minor passenger boarding delays or traffic congestion, trailing buses catch up because they encounter fewer waiting passengers. Consequently, multiple buses arrive simultaneously at stops, creating long gaps in service beforehand, severe passenger overcrowding, uneven crew workloads, and fuel inefficiencies.</p>
<p>To address these challenges, modern Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) require specialized depot-level software. C++ is the industry standard for performance-critical systems, providing deterministic memory management, zero-cost abstractions, object-oriented design, and rapid mathematical execution. By modeling physical fleet assets—such as buses, waypoints, routes, drivers, and supervisory alerts—as encapsulated C++ classes, the <strong>Bus Tracking and Management Application</strong> provides an integrated, robust, and extensible solution for depot managers and administrative staff.</p>

<h2>1.2 Problem Statement</h2>
<p>Regional bus depot administrations lack an integrated, lightweight digital platform to coordinate daily schedule allocations and track active fleet movements along designated transit routes. Manual recording often produces conflicting assignments of drivers, conductors, and vehicles, while supervisors have no automated mechanism to detect when vehicles on the same corridor converge dangerously close to each other.</p>
<p>The goal of this project is to design, model, and implement an Object-Oriented C++ application that allows office staff to assign daily route schedules (pairing bus registration numbers with routes, timings, drivers, and conductors), provides the depot manager with live tracking telemetry and crew records, and autonomously executes a spatial proximity algorithm to trigger real-time hazard alerts when buses on the same route operate within a critical proximity threshold.</p>

<h2>1.3 Objectives</h2>
<p>The primary objectives of this project are:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Functional Objective:</strong> To develop an integrated depot management application supporting bus registration, daily route assignment, fleet display, telemetry location updating, and same-route bus proximity checking.</li>
    <li><strong>Object-Oriented Design Objective:</strong> To design a clean class hierarchy utilizing core OOP tenets: abstract base classes (<code>Vehicle</code>), inheritance (<code>Bus</code>), runtime polymorphism via virtual functions, encapsulation, operator overloading, and generic templates.</li>
    <li><strong>Algorithmic Objective:</strong> To implement a real-time spatial proximity detection engine based on the spherical Haversine formula to identify vehicle bunching (&le; 0.75 km threshold) and broadcast immediate operational advisories.</li>
    <li><strong>Reliability &amp; Quality Objective:</strong> To guarantee system stability through robust input validation and custom exception handling (<code>TransitException</code>, <code>DuplicateBusException</code>, <code>DuplicateAllocationException</code>, <code>RouteNotFoundException</code>, <code>BusNotFoundException</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Persistence Objective:</strong> To implement structured file I/O operations for exporting daily operational schedules into standard comma-separated values (CSV) format.</li>
</ol>

<h2>1.4 Scope of the Project</h2>
<p><strong>In Scope:</strong></p>
<ul>
    <li>Interactive command-line interface featuring: 1. Register a bus, 2. Assign bus to a route, 3. Display registered buses, 4. Display route assignments, 5. Enter/update sample bus location, 6. Check same-route bus proximity, and 0. Exit.</li>
    <li>Dynamic daily route allocation associating vehicle registration numbers with routes, departure schedules, driver IDs, and conductor IDs.</li>
    <li>Master route catalog management with intermediate waypoints, distance indices, and geographical coordinates.</li>
    <li>Real-time fleet tracking displaying active vehicle positions, current stops, crew identities, and operational status.</li>
    <li>Pairwise spatial proximity calculation on co-routed buses to detect bunching and generate alerts.</li>
    <li>Telemetry location updating to simulate real-time bus movements.</li>
    <li>Robust exception handling and persistent export to CSV files.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Out of Scope:</strong></p>
<ul>
    <li>Physical hardware interfacing with in-vehicle OBD-II or GSM/cellular GPS modules (positions are modeled via telemetry feeds).</li>
    <li>Dynamic passenger ticketing, fare collection, and passenger-facing mobile applications.</li>
    <li>Relational SQL database servers (file handling and in-memory STL associative containers provide persistence).</li>
</ul>

<h2>1.5 Organisation of the Report</h2>
<p>Chapter 2 reviews existing transit management systems; Chapter 3 specifies requirements; Chapter 4 details system architecture, flowcharts, data structures, and the Haversine distance algorithm; Chapter 5 presents the complete Object-Oriented design and UML diagrams; Chapter 6 covers implementation with OOP mapping, C++17 features, and key code snippets; Chapter 7 demonstrates results, console screens, and test scenarios; Chapter 8 provides conclusions and future scope; Chapter 9 outlines individual team contributions, followed by References and Appendices.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<h1>CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE SURVEY AND EXISTING SYSTEMS</h1>

<h2>2.1 Overview of Fleet Management Systems</h2>
<p>Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) have evolved from static dispatch boards to automated vehicle location (AVL) systems. Modern fleet monitoring frameworks rely on spatial calculations to maintain scheduled headways. Literature on transit operations emphasizes that headway regularity is vastly more critical to passenger satisfaction than raw vehicle speed. When headways deteriorate, transit systems experience passenger accumulation cascades, leading to severe vehicle bunching.</p>

<h2>2.2 Comparison of Existing Solutions</h2>
<p class="caption">Table 1: Comparison of existing systems</p>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th>Sr.</th>
        <th>System / Source</th>
        <th>Key Features</th>
        <th>Limitations</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>1</td>
        <td><strong>Manual Depot Logbooks</strong> [1]</td>
        <td>No hardware or software overhead; simple paper-based recording of bus departures.</td>
        <td>High incidence of human error; zero live visibility; unable to detect route conflicts or bus bunching.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>2</td>
        <td><strong>Commercial GPS Web Portals</strong> [2]</td>
        <td>Raw latitude/longitude plotting; geofencing perimeter alerts; speed tracking.</td>
        <td>High recurring subscription costs; lack of integrated crew-to-schedule pairing; no automated route-aware bunching alerts.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>3</td>
        <td><strong>Enterprise Transit Suites</strong> [3]</td>
        <td>Automated scheduling; predictive dispatch; multi-modal passenger information systems.</td>
        <td>Prohibitive capital expenditure; steep learning curve; complex hardware dependencies unsuitable for lightweight depot operations.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>4</td>
        <td><strong>Proposed Bus Tracking Application</strong></td>
        <td>Unified depot console; automated schedule conflict validation; Haversine proximity engine; zero external dependencies; modern C++17 design.</td>
        <td>Relies on simulated telemetry feeds rather than integrated physical satellite modems.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

