<h2>7.2 Discussion and Interpretation</h2>
<p>The program successfully satisfies all problem requirements. When two buses on Route 101 approached within 0.352 km of each other, the proximity engine immediately detected the conflict against the 0.750 km threshold and generated a priority alert banner with speed regulation instructions. Duplicate allocation checks prevented double-booking of buses.</p>

<h2>7.3 Limitations</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Terminal-based text UI instead of a graphic map.</li>
    <li>Simulated telemetry rather than real hardware GPS feeds.</li>
    <li>Calculates great-circle distance rather than road-network turn-by-turn route distance.</li>
</ul>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<h1>CHAPTER 8: CONCLUSION AND FUTURE SCOPE</h1>

<h2>8.1 Conclusion</h2>
<p>The <strong>Bus Tracking and Management Application</strong> effectively resolves depot-level coordination challenges and bus bunching using an Object-Oriented C++17 architecture. The software establishes clear management of fleet rolling stock and daily schedules, guarantees data integrity via custom exceptions, and detects vehicle bunching in real time via the Haversine proximity engine.</p>

<h2>8.2 Future Scope</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>IoT Hardware Integration:</strong> Direct connection to in-vehicle OBD-II GPS transceivers via MQTT/HTTP.</li>
    <li><strong>Graphical GIS Interface:</strong> GUI desktop application using Qt / QML or OpenStreetMap.</li>
    <li><strong>Machine Learning:</strong> Predictive headway adjustment using historical traffic logs.</li>
    <li><strong>Passenger Mobile App:</strong> ETA announcements for waiting passengers.</li>
</ul>

